{"name": "isolated_graded_costly_l3", "outcome": "pruned", "detail": "ineligible: more than 4 SEs behind the best on E: 419.6 ± 100.3", "hypothesis": "Pragmatic listener at depth 3 with perceptual isolation, graded semantics, and utterance extension costs. Refines isolated_graded_costly_l2 by increasing the recursion depth from depth 2 to depth 3. Listeners reason at depth 3, inverting a speaker who simulates a depth-2 pragmatic listener. Visual pop-out is determined by an object's perceptual isolation (minimum Hamming distance to any other context object), semantic applicability diminishes with additional competing features (graded truth), and speakers evaluate communicative utility trade-offs with utterance extension costs proportional to the fraction of display objects sharing each feature. On uninformative prior trials, choice follows the perceptual isolation prior. A lapse parameter mixes in uniform guessing.", "context": "seed"}
{"name": "oddity_heuristic_listener", "outcome": "pruned", "detail": "ineligible: more than 4 SEs behind the best on E: 1773.0 ± 255.1", "hypothesis": "Perceptual oddity heuristic listener. Listeners interpret referring expressions using a perceptual oddity heuristic rather than recursive Theory of Mind. When hearing a word that applies to multiple referents, listeners select the contextually unique singleton that lacks identical duplicates in the visual display. On uninformative trials without an informative word, listeners spontaneously choose the unique singleton, defaulting to uniform choice when all items are distinct or no oddity contrast exists. A lapse parameter captures random clicking.", "context": "seed"}
{"name": "rsa_l1", "outcome": "pruned", "detail": "ineligible: more than 4 SEs behind the best on E: 620.5 ± 152.0", "hypothesis": "Vanilla RSA pragmatic listener (depth 1), uniform prior over objects. The listener reasons about a speaker who chooses a true word with probability proportional to exp(alpha * log L0(object | word)), where L0 is the literal listener; the listener inverts that speaker with a uniform prior over objects (Frank & Goodman, 2012). With no informative word the choice is uniform. A lapse parameter mixes in uniform guessing.", "context": "seed"}
{"name": "grammatical_exhaustification", "outcome": "pruned", "detail": "ineligible: more than 4 SEs behind the best on E: 364.2 ± 77.1", "hypothesis": "People resolve referring expressions using grammatical exhaustification over unasserted alternative descriptions rather than recursive Theory of Mind. When hearing a word, listeners rule out referents proportionally to the informativeness of any truer or more specific features of that referent that the speaker left unmentioned. On uninformative trials, visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 1 candidate 1 explore"}
{"name": "feature_parsimony_listener", "outcome": "pruned", "detail": "ineligible: more than 4 SEs behind the best on E: 478.0 ± 99.8", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions using a feature parsimony heuristic: among candidate referents satisfying a heard word, listeners choose the object possessing the fewest total visual features, assuming speakers provide minimal descriptions for simple targets rather than leaving distinguishing features unmentioned. Speakers communicate by selecting words that guide this parsimony-biased listener to the intended referent. On uninformative displays without an informative word, visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 3 candidate 1 explore"}
{"name": "structured_lapse_listener", "outcome": "pruned", "detail": "ineligible: more than 4 SEs behind the best on E: 440.7 ± 96.9", "hypothesis": "People separate communicative inference from response generation: listeners infer communicative intent using an unbiased, uniform target prior, and resolve their response through a salience-guided structured lapse that captures attention toward contextually unique visual singletons. On uninformative displays lacking a communicative word, choices default entirely to visual singleton salience; on informative trials, communicative beliefs are mixed at the decision level with the salience lapse rather than multiplied inside Bayes' rule.", "context": "round 3 candidate 2 explore"}
{"name": "competitor_confusion_speaker", "outcome": "pruned", "detail": "elpd_diff 637.0 > 2.0 x clustered dse 131.6 vs bounded_depth_listener on exp1 (grouped CV)", "hypothesis": "Pragmatic listener reasoning about a competitor-confusion-averse speaker. Speakers in reference games actively avoid referring expressions that are shared with visually similar or confusable distractors in the context. Candidate words are penalized proportionally to their feature overlap with alternative referents that also satisfy the word. Pragmatic listeners invert this confusion-averse speaker, expecting words to target referents whose competitors are visually distinct rather than near-identical clones.", "context": "seed"}
{"name": "rsa_l2", "outcome": "pruned", "detail": "elpd_diff 1034.8 > 2.0 x clustered dse 144.4 vs bounded_depth_listener on exp1 (grouped CV)", "hypothesis": "RSA pragmatic listener at depth 2, uniform prior over objects. One more level of recursion than the vanilla model: a speaker S2 chooses words by how well the depth-1 pragmatic listener L1 would recover the object (exp(alpha * log L1)), and the listener L2 inverts S2. Depth-2 reasoning is what the pragmods \"complex\" game needs to single out the object described by the twice-implicated word. One rationality alpha at every level; uniform choice with no informative word; a lapse parameter mixes in uniform guessing.", "context": "seed"}
{"name": "contextual_oddity_listener", "outcome": "pruned", "detail": "elpd_diff 662.9 > 2.0 x clustered dse 148.5 vs bounded_depth_listener on exp1 (grouped CV)", "hypothesis": "People interpret referring expressions by integrating communicative informativeness with contextual oddity salience. Before hearing an utterance, visual attention is captured by a solitary odd-one-out that breaks the homogeneous background of duplicate distractors, establishing a default referential prior over objects. Pragmatic listeners invert an informative speaker using this oddity-grounded referent prior, defaulting to uniform expectations when multiple distinct items or no oddities exist.", "context": "round 2 candidate 1 explore"}
{"name": "grammatical_exhaustification_distinct", "outcome": "pruned", "detail": "elpd_diff 151.2 > 2.0 x clustered dse 41.8 vs bounded_depth_listener on exp1 (grouped CV)", "hypothesis": "Refining grammatical_exhaustification by incorporating continuous contextual distinctiveness into the visual attention prior: listeners interpret referring expressions through grammatical exhaustification over unasserted alternative features, ruling out referents proportionally to the informativeness of any more specific descriptions the speaker omitted, while spontaneous visual attention weights referents by combining discrete singleton salience with continuous feature contrast (contextual distinctiveness) relative to display competitors. This directly improves the representation of displays lacking identical duplicates as well as empty singletons in full-duplicate contexts, addressing the empirical discrepancy where humans allocate substantial prior belief to empty singleton objects among identical full-feature duplicates. Differences from grammatical_exhaustification: recursion depth remains unchanged (depth 0, L_exh); parameter w_distinct is added with prior dist.Normal(0.0, 1.0); no parameters are removed; and the visual prior is extended by adding w_distinct times the total contextual feature contrast (summed Hamming distance across all context objects).", "context": "round 2 candidate 6 refine chosen"}
{"name": "bounded_depth_unbiased_prior", "outcome": "pruned", "detail": "elpd_diff 574.2 > 2.0 x clustered dse 144.2 vs bounded_depth_listener on exp1 (grouped CV)", "hypothesis": "Refining bounded_depth_listener by decoupling visual singleton salience from communicative inference: listeners interpret informative referring expressions with bounded reasoning depth under an unbiased, uniform referent prior, confining visual singleton salience strictly to spontaneous visual attention on uninformative displays without an informative word. This prevents spurious singleton bonuses from overriding scalar implicatures on informative trials, eliminating severe over-allocation to multi-feature singleton competitors when words are shared with duplicate minimal targets. Differences from bounded_depth_listener: recursion depth remains unchanged (depth 1, listener calls L1); no parameters are added or removed (retaining alpha and w_singleton); the prior vector parameter is removed from L1 so that the listener chooses referents proportionally to speaker choice probabilities alone; and listener calls L1 without the prior argument, restricting the singleton prior exclusively to prior trials.", "context": "round 3 candidate 4 refine incumbent bounded_depth_listener"}
{"name": "sortal_partition_listener", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: sortal_partition_listener: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "People interpret and produce referring expressions over sortal equivalence classes of objects rather than individual visual tokens. Communicative speakers choose words expecting listeners to identify referent types, while pragmatic listeners partition hypothesis weight across duplicate tokens within each sortal class, preventing identical distractors from spuriously diluting unique minimal referents. On uninformative displays without an informative word, visual attention defaults to contextually unique singletons.", "context": "round 1 candidate 1 explore"}
{"name": "type_level_reference", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: type_level_reference: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "People interpret and produce referring expressions by reasoning over perceptual equivalence classes (referential types) rather than raw object tokens: identical visual objects in a scene are perceptually grouped into a single type, and communicative speakers choose utterances to distinguish the target's type from alternative types. Pragmatic listeners invert this type-discriminating speaker while maintaining spontaneous visual attention on contextually unique singletons. In displays where a heard word is universally true of all objects and distractors are identical duplicates, this mechanism predicts listeners will strongly attribute the word to the visual singleton, disagreeing sharply with standard token-based models that impose spurious M-implicature penalties on singletons.", "context": "round 1 candidate 2 explore"}
{"name": "contrast_deficit_speaker", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: contrast_deficit_speaker: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Speakers choose referring expressions to maximize contextual contrast, penalizing descriptions that leave avoidable ambiguity when a more distinguishing alternative is available for the target. When an object has no distinguishing features, speakers fall back to general descriptions without penalty. Pragmatic listeners invert this contrast-sensitive speaker, attributing underspecified words to referents that lack more specific alternatives.", "context": "round 1 candidate 3 explore"}
{"name": "rsa_l1_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0_2", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "hypothesis.md is word for word rsa_l1_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0's hypothesis, or part of it. State this candidate's own mechanism, including what it changes from the theory it refines.", "hypothesis": "RSA pragmatic listener at depth 1 with singleton salience, contextual distinctiveness, visual feature complexity, evaluative valence framing, visual color contrast, and literal L0.", "context": "round 1 candidate 4 refine incumbent rsa_l1_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0"}
{"name": "rsa_l1_shared_perceptual_prior", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: rsa_l1_shared_perceptual_prior: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions by inverting an informative speaker who models a foundational listener sharing common ground of perceptual salience, so that visual salience directly informs communicative expectations rather than being applied solely as a downstream selection bias. When hearing a word that applies universally across a scene, the speaker's expectation that the listener will naturally attend to visually unique singletons prevents a spurious M-implicature penalty against the singleton, correctly aligning predicted ratings with human responses on duplicate displays. Refining rsa_l1_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0 by making the contextual perceptual prior common knowledge in the foundational simulated listener L0: recursion depth remains depth 1 (listener calls L1); no parameters are added or removed (retaining alpha, w_singleton, w_distinct, w_features, w_valence, and w_color); L0 adds the prior vector to its signature and weights referents by the product of the prior vector and semantic truth; and in L1 the speaker evaluates L0 with the prior argument.", "context": "round 1 candidate 5 refine incumbent rsa_l1_singleton_feat_color_valence_l0"}
{"name": "bounded_depth_graded_truth", "outcome": "rejected", "detail": "fitting failed: bounded_depth_graded_truth: the fit did not finish within 30 minutes", "hypothesis": "Listeners interpret referring expressions with strictly bounded reasoning depth (depth 1) by inverting an informative communicative speaker, but evaluate semantic applicability as graded truth that diminishes with each additional competing visual feature. Refining bounded_depth_listener by incorporating graded semantic applicability from isolated_graded_costly_l3 into the literal semantics: candidate referents with additional unmentioned visual features possess lower baseline applicability, enabling listeners to strongly favor minimal referents when words are universally shared while maintaining visual singleton salience on uninformative displays. Differences from bounded_depth_listener: recursion depth remains depth 1 (listener calls L1); parameter gamma is added with prior dist.Normal(0.0, 1.0); no parameters are removed (retaining alpha and w_singleton); literal lexicon applicability is scaled by exp(-gamma * (feature_count - 1)) for real utterances; and the listener passes this graded lexicon into L1 on informative trials.", "context": "round 1 candidate 6 refine chosen"}
